export anchor OpenQuestions:
    limits:
        - The values the operator sets at launch for the free allowance and for each plan
    models:
        - Which model sits behind each effort level at launch
    sourcesToVerify:
        - The public root zone list of top level domains, its address and its refresh rate
        - The public bootstrap file that maps a registry to its registration query service
        - The registration query service address for the .com registry, and its rate limits
        - The terms of use of each of these sources, and whether a hosted product may query them at this volume
        - Whether a paid lookup service is worth adding for speed, and which one
        - How long an answer stays correct enough to serve from cache
    seed:
        - Which products and industries seed a new project so the first round has material
        - Whether the admin interface holds a dictionary of word roots and language styles
    domains:
        - The length of the freshness window for a stored result
    money:
        - The price of Pro per month and per year, and the currency
        - The Studio per-seat price, and the size of the per-seat discount
        - Whether a year paid up front costs less than twelve months
        - The size and price of credit packs
        - How far a plan that has ended still covers revision rounds and domain checks, which the plan's counters normally pay for
        - What is charged when a Studio organization drops below three seats
        - Which countries Stripe is used to sell in, and which tax rules apply
    accounts:
        - Whether a third organization role, such as an admin, is needed for the first version
        - How the choice between two copies of the same project is presented
        - Whether the person can merge two projects instead of choosing one
    data:
        - Where the stored work lives, which database and which file store
        - Whether a copy of the work is kept in more than one region
        - How many days a backup is kept before the 30-day deletion window starts
    delivery:
        - The technology stack for the application
        - Where the application is hosted and which region holds the data
    email:
        - The transactional email provider and the sending address
        - How long a magic link stays valid
        - Whether an invitation email is resent automatically, and how often
    failures:
        - The length of the short window in which a repeat raises an alert, and how long an alert stays open
        - Which parts the operator can mark unavailable, and whether a person is told when a part that was marked unavailable returns

// Working state for the open-question interview. Not exported, so it is not
// compiled; it is removed when the person accepts the result.
anchor DeepDiveState:
    target: The open questions listed under OpenQuestions
    budget: 3 questions in this round
    answered:
        - Credits never expire and stay usable after a subscription ends
        - A long provider outage fails each round plainly, never queues it, and the level returns by itself
        - A name that cannot form a valid .com domain is shown as not valid, with a plain reason, and spends no check
    focus: Refinement is complete for this round
    refinementApplied: true
    awaiting: The choice between accepting the result and continuing

export anchor SpecificationState:
    accepted:
        The person accepted this specification as good enough for a first
        version. It is the current description of the product. The five
        interview rounds that built it are finished, and nothing here is
        waiting on a further interview.
    settled:
        - The product is a web application and paid service at nametempest.com
        - The first version serves solo founders, small-business owners and agencies at the same time
        - The first version generates names and checks .com domain availability
        - The free allowance ships at 5 rounds of 12 names, 3 revisions and 20 checks, and the operator may change it
        - Free use needs no account and is counted per browser, with a looser ceiling per network address
        - Signing in attaches the work to the account, and the account's own counters apply from then on
        - Sign-in is an email address with a password, or an email magic link
        - There are two plans, Pro for one person and Studio for at least three seats, with Studio the higher tier because it adds organization controls, plus optional credit top-ups
        - Bought credits never expire and stay usable after a subscription ends
        - Stripe takes the payment and holds the card
        - The first version includes shared projects, live editing and owner and member roles inside a Studio organization
        - There are three effort levels, Quick, Balanced and Thorough, costing 1, 2 and 3 rounds, and the operator may change those multipliers
        - A domain check proves a name taken from DNS and proves a name free only from a registration query
        - A name that cannot form a valid .com domain is shown as not valid, with a plain reason, and spends no check
        - Every limit and price is a setting in the operator's admin interface, never a number fixed in the specification
        - Email carries only a magic link, an invitation, a receipt and an admin notice, never marketing
        - Work is stored while the project exists, can be exported as a file, and is gone 30 days after deletion
        - A failure gives a plain message, one retry and no charge, and a repeat reaches the operator
        - A long provider outage fails rounds one at a time, never queues them, and the level returns by itself
        - The first month is judged on the behaviour targets, plus three numbers the operator sets before launch
    open:
        - Every item under OpenQuestions
    howToContinue:
        The open questions are launch decisions, not gaps in behaviour. Each
        is settled by a live check or by the operator writing a value before
        launch. A later round of questions should start from OpenQuestions
        and leave the requirements above untouched unless the person changes
        a decision.
